nchmark Checks
Benchmark checks involve comparing an estimated quantity produced by the model against an independent estimate of the same or similar quantity. These checks are opportunistic, rather than systematic, in that they depend on the availability of an independent dataset to verify against. Examples of checks include: 
Comparing fire emissions per hectare against expert opinion or values found in the literature; 
Comparing the age-class structure for forest inventories of the same area; 
Comparing dead organic matter density (C stocks per hectare) against expert opinion or values found in regional or national soils databases; 
Comparing net primary productivity (NPP) and net ecosystem productivity (NEP) against published values for similar ecosystems;
Back-calculating biomass expansion factors to compare against values reported in the literature; 
Comparing biomass expansion factors and emissions ratios with defaults reported in the IPCC 2003 Good Practice Guidance.

The objective of these checks is to confirm that model inputs and outputs are consistent with values or ranges of values reported elsewhere. A model calibration exercise might be recommended in the event that a model input or output falls outside an observed range.
Historically these checks have occurred continuously as part of scientific rigour and while some have been published in the scientific literature, not all have been formally documented as part of a QAQC system.
External Reviews
External reviews are conducted regularly with data providers to ensure that their data have been interpreted correctly and that any novel interpretations of their data are consistent with the providers’ understanding of the data. Any issues that arise from these reviews are documented for subsequent follow-up.
As the central federal government GHG Inventory agency under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), Environment Canada also does a very detailed review of the annual forest GHG inventory estimates and of land class transfers between the forest, agricultural, and other sectors, prior to incorporating them in the annual National Inventory Report. Environment Canada’s quality control review process (EC, 2011; EC, 2007) is a critical component of the overall NFCMARS quality control system. Any issues reported by Environment Canada are either corrected prior to finalizing the current inventory report, or are recorded for investigation and resolution in a subsequent NIR, depending on the severity of the issue.
The NFCMARS was also reviewed as part of the 2007 in-country review conducted by the UNFCCC secretariat in accordance with Guidelines for review under Article 8 of the Kyoto Protocol (decision 22/CMP.1).
