Methodological Updates
New CBM-CFS3 executable
This update consolidates several software improvements into the core CBM-CFS3 executable. The software improvements included are:
code corrections to resolve differences in model results when running on Windows10 versus Windows7;
code corrections to the initialization of C pools prior to afforestation activity; 
enhanced diagnostics. 
Revised volume to biomass conversion parameters for BC
This improvement project replaces the BC volume-to-biomass conversion parameters that were implemented in NIR2017 with corrected values.
“All the procedures used to develop the most recent version of the CFS volume-to-biomass models for B.C. (2014) were reviewed during the spring of 2018, as part of a larger comparison between BCMFLNR and CFS volume-to-biomass models.
During the review, it was discovered that the compilation of the older ‘S’ type plots in the biomass database still had some issues (these ‘S’ type plots are large, temporary sample plots used for inventory in B.C. during the 1950’s, 1960’s and 1970’s - there are over 20,000 in the database). Most of them are actually a cluster of 2 separate plots, but are combined and treated as 1 large plot for analysis and modeling. However, during this phase, the individual plot areas were not combined and ended up being half the size in the final database (e.g. plot area of 0.08 ha was used instead of 0.16 ha). 
The compilation procedures have been revised to combine the plot areas correctly, and all the B.C. volume-to-biomass models have been updated (May, 2018). An overall effect of this change on the models is to reduce the biomass they estimate by a small amount, usually < 10%. However, some ecozone-species combinations that contain a high proportion of ‘S’ plots have greater changes (e.g. white pine stands can show a 20% or more reduction in estimated biomass), or even have small increases in biomass estimates.” – P. Boudewyn, personal communication.
